##  # ---------- Calibration anchors ----------
##  # SALT-1/SALT-2 (Schrier 2006 NEJM)          : tolvaptan +3-4 mEq/L Na at day 4
##  #   vs placebo, sustained to day 30; modeled via EC50_TOLVA/EMAX_TOLVA on
##  #   AQP2 target with slow KOUT_NA relaxation.
##  # Conivaptan (Ghali 2006; Verbalis 2008)       : similar/faster Na rise given
##  #   IV loading + continuous infusion (higher initial exposure than oral vaptan).
##  # Demeclocycline (Forrest 1978; De Troyer 1977): slower onset (3-5 days) than
##  #   vaptans, modeled via lower KOUT_DEMEC; effective in chronic SIADH,
##  #   historically used when vaptans contraindicated/unavailable (hepatotoxicity
##  #   risk with vaptans in cirrhosis is why demeclocycline persists as an option).
##  # Correction-rate guidelines (Verbalis 2013 Am J Med; Spasovski 2014 Eur J
##  #   Endocrinol)                               : <=8 mEq/L/24h (<=4-6 if
##  #   high-risk) and <=18 mEq/L/48h; LIMIT_24H triggers ODS_RISK accrual only
##  #   above threshold, scaled by cerebral-adaptation vulnerability.
##  # Osmotic demyelination syndrome (Sterns 1986; Sterns 2010)  : risk is
##  #   negligible in ACUTE hyponatremia (BRAIN_OSM near normal, low
##  #   vulnerability) even with fast correction, and highest in CHRONIC
##  #   hyponatremia corrected too fast (BRAIN_OSM depleted -> high vulnerability)
##  #   -- reproduced via the vulnerability term gating K_ODS.
##
